BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//SimpleAct//eu-ai-act-dataset//EN
CALSCALE:GREGORIAN
X-WR-CALNAME:EU AI Act: application dates
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-entry-2024-08-01@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20240801
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20240802
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: AI Act enters into force
DESCRIPTION:Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 is in force. No obligations apply yet
  – the date starts the staggered transition periods.\n\nLegal basis: Art
 . 113
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-bans-2025-02-02@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20250202
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20250203
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: Prohibited practices and AI literacy
DESCRIPTION:The Art. 5 prohibitions apply\, e.g. social scoring\, manipulat
 ive techniques\, emotion recognition at work and untargeted scraping of fa
 cial images. From the same day\, providers and deployers must take measure
 s on their staff’s AI literacy (Art. 4).\n\nLegal basis: Art. 4\, Art. 5
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-gpai-2025-08-02@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20250802
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20250803
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: General-purpose AI models
DESCRIPTION:Obligations for GPAI model providers apply: technical documenta
 tion\, information for downstream providers\, a copyright policy and a tra
 ining-data summary\; for systemic-risk models additional evaluation and ri
 sk mitigation. The governance structure and the penalty framework apply as
  well.\n\nLegal basis: Ch. V\, Ch. VII\, Art. 99
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-omnibus-2026-07-27@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260727
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260728
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: Digital Omnibus enters into force
DESCRIPTION:Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 moves the high-risk deadlines and rew
 rites Art. 4: companies must take measures to promote AI literacy but no l
 onger guarantee a specific level per person. SMEs and small mid-caps get r
 elief on documentation and quality management.\n\nLegal basis: Reg. (EU) 2
 026/1744
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-general-2026-08-02@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260802
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260803
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: General application and transparency obligations
DESCRIPTION:The Regulation applies in general. The Art. 50 transparency obl
 igations kick in: AI that interacts with people must say so\, deepfakes an
 d emotion recognition must be disclosed\, and newly placed generative syst
 ems must mark their outputs in a machine-readable way. From now on the Com
 mission can fine GPAI providers.\n\nLegal basis: Art. 50\, Art. 101\, Art.
  113
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-dec2026-2026-12-02@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261202
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261203
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: End of the marking transition\, new prohibitions
DESCRIPTION:Generative AI systems already on the market before 2 August 202
 6 must now mark their outputs in a machine-readable way too (Art. 50(2)). 
 Two new prohibitions apply: AI that creates or manipulates intimate depict
 ions of identifiable people without consent\, and AI that generates child 
 sexual abuse material.\n\nLegal basis: Art. 50(2)\, Art. 5 (new)
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-aug2027-2027-08-02@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270802
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270803
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: Legacy GPAI models and AI sandboxes
DESCRIPTION:GPAI models placed on the market before 2 August 2025 must meet
  the Chapter V obligations. Every Member State must run at least one natio
 nal AI regulatory sandbox – the Omnibus pushed this back by a year.\n\nL
 egal basis: Art. 111(3)\, Art. 57
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-annex3-2027-12-02@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20271202
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20271203
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: High-risk AI under Annex III
DESCRIPTION:The high-risk requirements (Chapter III\, Sections 1 to 3) appl
 y to stand-alone systems under Annex III – e.g. in employment\, educatio
 n\, creditworthiness\, critical infrastructure and access to essential ser
 vices. Providers need risk management\, technical documentation\, conformi
 ty assessment and registration\; deployers must ensure human oversight and
  keep logs\, among other duties (Art. 26).\n\nLegal basis: Art. 6(2)\, Ann
 ex III\, Art. 8–27
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-annex1-2028-08-02@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20280802
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20280803
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: High-risk AI in regulated products (Annex I)
DESCRIPTION:AI systems that are a safety component of a product under the A
 nnex I harmonisation legislation\, or are such a product themselves – e.
 g. machinery\, medical devices\, toys or lifts – must meet the high-risk
  requirements. Before the Omnibus this was August 2027.\n\nLegal basis: Ar
 t. 6(1)\, Annex I
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eu-ai-act-public-2030-08-02@simpleact.de
DTSTAMP:20261124T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20300802
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20300803
SUMMARY:EU AI Act: Legacy high-risk AI at public authorities
DESCRIPTION:Providers and deployers of high-risk AI systems intended for us
 e by public authorities must comply by now at the latest – even if the s
 ystems were in use before.\n\nLegal basis: Art. 111(2)
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
